 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [VoxelBlur Depth-of-Field Volume Rendering](10657-voxelblur-depth-of-field-volume-rendering.md)
-VoxelBlur is an interactive direct volume rendering method that provides a depth-of-field effect by selectively blurring voxels based on their distance from a predefined focus plane. It extends view-aligned slicing by splitting the rendering pipeline into front-to-back and back-to-front passes, each maintaining an intermediate buffer where blur is applied during compositing. Unlike standard back-to-front blending which relies on native GPU blending, VoxelBlur uses custom shader-based blending to enable the blur effect, allowing users to enhance depth perception and separate focus from context.
+An interactive method extending view-aligned slicing by splitting the pipeline into front-to-back and back-to-front passes, using custom shader-based blending to apply blur based on distance from a focus plane.
 
 ### Object-Aligned Slicing
-Creates three stacks of 2D textures corresponding to the x, y, and z principal directions [S1, unofficial]. The texture stack is swapped based on the viewpoint's closest principal direction [S1, unofficial]. It is fast and simple but causes 3x memory consumption, data replication, image popping artifacts during swaps, lag while downloading new textures, and intensity variations as the camera moves [S1, unofficial].
+Uses three stacks of 2D textures for x, y, z directions, swapping the stack based on the viewpoint's closest principal direction. It is faster but suffers from 3x memory consumption and image popping artifacts [S1, unofficial].
 
 ### Arbitrary Slicing
-Uses a plane as proxy geometry to sample arbitrary planes for removing depth complexity and exploring volumes [S4]. The strategy involves rotating the view so the arbitrary plane is parallel to the image plane (view-aligned), rendering the element to the back buffer, and displaying the result in a separate viewport or as a textured polygon in object space [S4].
+Uses a plane as proxy geometry to sample arbitrary planes for removing depth complexity. The plane is view-aligned and rendered to the back buffer [S5].
 
 ### Ray-to-Gravity-Center (R-GC) Volume Slicing
-A volume slicing strategy where parallel cutting planes are oriented perpendicular to the straight line connecting the point of reflection to the gravity center of the volume domain, used to generate Layered Polygonal Area Lights (LPALs) [S5]. Compared to Ray-of-Regular-Reflection (R-RR) slicing, R-GC ensures LPALs face properly to the point of reflection regardless of the regular reflection direction, correctly rendering the upper part of the reflected appearance when the reflection ray is apart from the volume [S5].
+A strategy where parallel cutting planes are oriented perpendicular to the line connecting the point of reflection to the gravity center of the volume, used for Layered Polygonal Area Lights (LPALs) [S6].
 
 ### GPU Volume Rendering Implementation
-A rendering algorithm running on the GPU based on 3D texture sampling using view-aligned slices as proxy geometry [S6, unofficial]. Volume data is stored in GPU memory as an 8-bit 3D texture, transfer functions as 256-entry lookup tables, and pre-integrated tables as 2D RGBA textures [S6, unofficial]. It supports two pre-integration levels: simplified (neglecting color attenuation, faster) for initial interaction, and full (without compromising interactivity) for detailed editing [S6, unofficial]. Volume shading can be enabled via a per-fragment Phong lighting model using scalar field gradients as normals [S6, unofficial]. The slice count is user-adjustable and automatically reduced during rotation to guarantee interactive rates [S6, unofficial].
+A GPU-based algorithm using view-aligned slices with two levels of pre-integration (simplified and full) and optional Phong lighting [S7, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-- **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-- **Measured Performance:** Slower than static ray casting for fixed viewpoints due to geometry regeneration, but more memory efficient [S1, unofficial]. Consistent sampling distance is maintained relative to the camera [S1, unofficial].
-- **Scaling:** Number of slices is user-adjustable and automatically reduced during rotation to guarantee interactive rates [S6, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Consistent sampling distance regardless of viewpoint; leverages standard GPU rasterization pipeline; easy to integrate with other view-aligned proxy geometry operations (e.g., picking) [S1, unofficial][S4].
-- **Limitations:** Geometry must be regenerated on view change; potential for sampling artifacts if slice spacing is too large relative to volume features; requires careful handling of texture clamping [S3, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** Interactive exploration where consistent sampling density is critical; scenarios requiring integration with other view-aligned proxy geometry operations (e.g., arbitrary slicing, picking) [S4].
-- **When not to use:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot S)$, where $N$ is the number of slices and $S$ is the number of vertices per slice (constant for quads). Geometry generation is $O(N)$.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V + S \cdot N)$, where $V$ is volume texture size and $S$ is slice geometry size.
+*   **Performance:** Interactive rates are achievable if the number of slices is reduced during rotation [S7, unofficial]. Consistent sampling distance is maintained [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Consistent sampling distance, memory efficient compared to object-aligned slicing [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Slower than object-aligned slicing due to per-frame geometry regeneration [S1, unofficial]. Requires dynamic buffer updates [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-View-Aligned Slicing is a proxy geometry-based volume rendering technique. It was discussed in the context of integrating dynamic deformations into interactive volume visualization, highlighting the flexibility of using proxy geometry for various sampling strategies including ray-casting and arbitrary slicing [S4]. Implemented in GPU-based tools using 3D texture sampling with view-aligned slices [S6, unofficial]. It builds on the principles of slice-based rendering but emphasizes dynamic alignment with the view to maintain sampling consistency [S1, unofficial].
+Originating from research on integrating dynamic deformations into interactive volume visualization, view-aligned slicing uses proxy geometry to sample volumes [S5]. It is implemented in various educational and thesis projects [S2, S3, S4, S7, unofficial] and slides [S1, unofficial].
 
